  "item": "impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This required SourceProvider callback is an error guard: the fixture\n supplies cached snapshots by overriding parse. Structural search must\n use those snapshots without refetching. Calling read would fail the\n contract tested below; primitive reads are exercised by other providers."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/sources.rs#impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` meets the built-in's criteria.\n\n- **Form:** It sits on an explicitly declared function with a body. The justification is in fenced citation commentary, not a bare comment.\n- **Substance:** The commentary gives a real reason. `read` is a required `SourceProvider` method with no default (`src/sources.rs#trait[SourceProvider]/fn[read]`). The fixture `CachedParse` overrides `parse` to return a cached snapshot. Its `read` deliberately returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"cached parser must not refetch\")`, so any refetch would fail.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The test `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` calls `search_structure` three times. Each call `.unwrap()`s the result, and the test asserts `parses == 3`. This confirms search goes through the overridden `parse` and never reaches `read`. That is why `read` records no execution. The claim that other providers exercise primitive reads is plausible, since `LocalSources` implements `read` as a real read.\n- **Scope:** The exception excuses only this function's zero execution, which is what the built-in permits.",
